response.text:
[
  {
    "question": "What are the main subproblems into which the general problem of simulating intelligence has been broken?",
    "answer": "The general problem of simulating intelligence has been broken into subproblems consisting of particular traits or capabilities that researchers expect an intelligent system to display, such as reasoning and problem-solving, knowledge representation, planning and decision-making, and learning.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 2,
    "citations": ["The general problem of simulating (or creating) intelligence has been broken into subproblems. These consist of particular traits or capabilities that researchers expect an intelligent system to display."]
  },
  {
    "question": "What limitation did early AI algorithms for reasoning and problem-solving face when dealing with large problems?",
    "answer": "Early AI algorithms for reasoning and problem-solving became exponentially slower as problems grew, a phenomenon known as a \"combinatorial explosion,\" making them insufficient for solving large reasoning problems.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 3,
    "citations": ["Many of these algorithms are insufficient for solving large reasoning problems because they experience a \"combinatorial explosion\": They become exponentially slower as the problems grow."]
  },
  {
    "question": "What are reasoning models, and what improvement did they bring in 2024?",
    "answer": "Reasoning models are a type of large language model trained to generate intermediate chains-of-thought. They emerged in 2024 and allowed improved performance on complex problems in mathematics and coding.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 4,
    "citations": ["Reasoning models, a type of large language model trained to generate intermediate chains-of-thought, emerged in 2024 and allowed improved performance on complex problems in mathematics and coding."]
  },
  {
    "question": "What is a knowledge base in the context of AI, and what does it need to represent?",
    "answer": "A knowledge base is a body of knowledge represented in a form that can be used by a program. It needs to represent things such as objects, properties, categories, relations between objects, situations, events, states, time, causes and effects, knowledge about knowledge, default reasoning, and many other aspects and domains of knowledge.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 5,
    "citations": ["A knowledge base is a body of knowledge represented in a form that can be used by a program.", "Knowledge bases need to represent things such as objects, properties, categories, and relations between objects; situations, events, states, and time; causes and effects; knowledge about knowledge (what we know about what other people know); default reasoning (things that humans assume are true until they are told differently and will remain true even when other facts are changing); and many other aspects and domains of knowledge."]
  },
  {
    "question": "What are some of the most difficult problems in knowledge representation?",
    "answer": "Among the most difficult problems in knowledge representation are the breadth of commonsense knowledge (the enormous set of atomic facts that the average person knows), the sub-symbolic form of most commonsense knowledge (much of what people know is not expressed verbally as facts or statements), and the difficulty of knowledge acquisition (obtaining knowledge for AI applications).",
    "estimated_difficulty": 7,
    "citations": ["Among the most difficult problems in knowledge representation are the breadth of commonsense knowledge (the set of atomic facts that the average person knows is enormous); and the sub-symbolic form of most commonsense knowledge (much of what people know is not represented as \"facts\" or \"statements\" that they could express verbally). There is also the difficulty of knowledge acquisition, the problem of obtaining knowledge for AI applications."]
  },
  {
    "question": "In automated decision-making, how does a rational agent choose an action?",
    "answer": "In automated decision-making, a rational agent assigns a utility number to each situation, calculates the expected utility for each possible action (the utility of all possible outcomes weighted by their probability), and then chooses the action with the maximum expected utility.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 6,
    "citations": ["The decision-making agent assigns a number to each situation (called its \"utility\") that measures how much the agent prefers it. For each possible action, it can calculate the \"expected utility\": the utility of all possible outcomes of the action, weighted by the probability that the outcome will occur. It can then choose the action with the maximum expected utility."]
  },
  {
    "question": "What is the difference between classical planning and most real-world planning problems?",
    "answer": "In classical planning, the agent knows exactly what the effect of any action will be. In most real-world problems, the agent may not be certain about the situation it is in (it is unknown or unobservable) and may not know for certain what will happen after each possible action (it is not deterministic), requiring probabilistic guesses and reassessment.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 6,
    "citations": ["In classical planning, the agent knows exactly what the effect of any action will be. In most real-world problems, however, the agent may not be certain about the situation it is in (it is \"unknown\" or \"unobservable\") and it may not know for certain what will happen after each possible action (it is not \"deterministic\"). It must choose an action by making a probabilistic guess and then reassess the situation to see if the action worked."]
  },
  {
    "question": "What is a Markov decision process composed of?",
    "answer": "A Markov decision process has a transition model that describes the probability that a particular action will change the state in a particular way and a reward function that supplies the utility of each state and the cost of each action.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 8,
    "citations": ["A Markov decision process has a transition model that describes the probability that a particular action will change the state in a particular way and a reward function that supplies the utility of each state and the cost of each action."]
  },
  {
    "question": "What is machine learning, and how has it been related to AI?",
    "answer": "Machine learning is the study of programs that can improve their performance on a given task automatically. It has been a part of AI from the beginning.",
    "estimated_difficulty": 2,
    "citations": ["Machine learning is the study of programs that can improve their performance on a given task automatically. It has been a part of AI from the beginning."]
  }
]

